  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nClouds are on the increase across the area from southwest to\nnortheast as a mid-level shortwave and low-level warm front\napproach from the west. A resulting area of showers is expanding\nnortheastward from the Mid-Atlantic, while vigorous convection \nis ongoing upstream in the Ohio Valley. Another mid-level \nshortwave to our north has helped to initiate some showers over \nthe international border, but both of these disturbances will \nremain displaced from our region through tomorrow. Therefore, \nwhile we continue to expect scattered showers this afternoon\nthrough tonight due to warm air advection and adjacent forcing \nfor ascent, the amount of rainfall we will see has decreased\nfurther. Likely also contributing to our lower QPF is the \naforementioned convection upstream. Heavy rain is pouring into \nthe Ohio Valley and likely robbing us of some of our available \nmoisture. In fact, our current dewpoint depression spread ranges\nfrom about 13-20 degrees. So the showers that are currently \nincoming are going to have some dry air to battle before \nreaching the ground.\n\nThat said, as the warm sector of the system moves into our area\nthis evening into tonight and moisture increases, the column\nwill moisten, showers will become a bit more widespread, and\nrain will actually start reaching the ground more consistently.\nOverall, we anticipate anywhere from about 0.1\" to 0.25\" this\nafternoon through tonight with an additional 0.05\" to 0.2\"\nthrough tomorrow afternoon as the still departing system\ncontinues to cause highly scattered showers and possibly a \nthunderstorm or two. However, as rainfall rates are anticipated\nto be very light and the progressive nature of this system will\nlimit shower/storm duration over single locations, there are no\nconcerns of flooding today or tomorrow. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA period of dry conditions returns Tuesday before the chances for\nshowers and potential thunderstorms increase Wednesday ahead of\na cold front. At this time, there is not an expectation for\nany storms to become severe as instability appears limited amid\nmeager lapse rates. That said, deep layer shear is about 40 kt\nso storm organization cannot be ruled out. The biggest factor at\nplay will be the timing of the front.",
